data: {"step": "response", "status": "streaming", "detail": "**Unusual patterns and points that merit close monitoring**\n\n1. **Relationship information is sparse and uneven** \u2013 only **10 FIRs** record any relationship between the accused and the complainant/victim, yet those entries total **24 relationship records** across those FIRs. The dominant relationship is **\u0627\u062c\u0646\u0628\u06cc (stranger)**, noted **15 times** in **6 FIRs**; the remaining relationships (e.g., **\u0633\u0627\u0633 (mother\u2011in\u2011law)**, **\u0633\u06cc\u0646\u0626\u0631 \u0633\u0627\u062a\u06be\u06cc \u06a9\u0627\u0631 (senior co\u2011worker)**, **\u0634\u0648\u06c1\u0631 (husband)**, **\u0645\u062d\u0644\u06d2 \u062f\u0627\u0631 (neighbour)**, **\u0628\u06be\u0627\u0626\u06cc (brother)**) appear only once or twice each. Moreover, relationships are recorded for just **12 of 92 distinct accused**\u202f[Document\u202f1].\n\n2. **Seized property handling shows limited forensic follow\u2011up** \u2013 **28 FIRs** contain seized\u2011property entries, amounting to **45 property\u2011register entries**. Only **13 items** (in **11 FIRs**) were **DISPATCHED to a forensic laboratory**, while **7 items** (in **7 FIRs**) are slated **to be handed over to the heirs** of a deceased person\u202f[Document\u202f2].\n\n3. **Significant gaps in basic case documentation** \u2013 among **73 FIRs**, **9 FIRs** lack an incident date, and **52 FIRs** have no recorded investigation status, meaning many cases cannot be placed on a timeline or tracked through their lifecycle\u202f[Document\u202f3].\n\n4. **Recovered weapons are largely undocumented for licensing** \u2013 of the **32 weapon\u2011register entries**, **30 recovered weapons** are recorded **without a licence**, and an additional **2 entries** have **no licence status at all**. This suggests a high prevalence of unlicensed weapon recovery\u202f[Document\u202f4].\n\n5. **Shift in case mix indicates emerging legal concerns** \u2013 compared with 2024, **PPC (Pakistan Penal Code) cases** have risen sharply from **13** to **39** in 2026, while **Arms Ordinance\u202f1965** cases have slipped slightly from **13** to **16**. New categories appearing in 2026 include **CNSA\u202f1997 (12 cases)**, **PECA\u202f2016 (9 cases)**, **Punjab Domestic Violence Act (4 cases)**, and **Illegal Dispossession Act\u202f2005 (2 cases)**, pointing to a diversification toward domestic\u2011violence, economic\u2011crime and land\u2011dispute legislation\u202f[Document\u202f5].\n\nThese observations\u2014limited relationship documentation, low forensic processing of seized items, extensive missing case fields, a high number of unlicensed weapons, and a notable shift toward PPC and newer statutes\u2014should be flagged for further investigation and possible procedural reinforcement.\n\n_Could not answer sub-question (timed out): How many cases involve an accused person, and what is their age range and average age, across all cases?_\n_Could not answer sub-question (timed out): How many cases record an incident time, and at what time of day do those incidents happen, across all cases?_\n_Could not answer sub-question (timed out): How many cases does each accused person appear in, and which FIR numbers, across all cases?_\n_The natural-language summary could not be verified as an accurate paraphrase; showing the raw computed aggregate instead._\n_Could not answer sub-question (timed out): How many cases in the criminal record system have a court outcome that matches the recorded conviction status, across all cases?_"}

